From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:24:06 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/smmstore: Erase the commit block of a reclaim only
 when it is not

Measured on a ThinkPad W541 with the option lock armed: a reclaim took
8.9 to 9.2 s before the previous patch and 8.65 to 8.70 s after it. The
writes skipped and the read back through the mapping were not where the
time goes: the erases of blocks of 64 KiB (SMMSTORE_BLOCK_SIZE) are,
about a second each on that flash, eight of them a reclaim: three of
the staging area by the payload, three of the store, and the commit
block before the record and after the copy.

The commit block is erased at the end of every reclaim that completes,
so the next one finds it erased: read it through the mapping first and
erase it only if something is there (a record of a cut reclaim is
handled by ramstage before, a note of a refusal is not erased). One
erase fewer a reclaim. The comment says where the time goes.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Change-Id: I3e7576c9f82f8d1a28fae9998a2eb30cb611e9d7
---
 src/drivers/smmstore/reclaim.c | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/drivers/smmstore/reclaim.c b/src/drivers/smmstore/reclaim.c
index 6985fa403d7..13c91a5ddce 100644
--- a/src/drivers/smmstore/reclaim.c
+++ b/src/drivers/smmstore/reclaim.c
@@ -34,7 +34,10 @@
  * back, at most SMMSTORE_RECLAIM_PER_BOOT times a boot and only while the
  * store is nearly full. With every block written and read back through the
  * writable device it took 2.3 s for 192 KiB on a ThinkCentre M920x and 9 s on
- * a ThinkPad W541. A swap split over several SMIs would leave a half-written store
+ * a ThinkPad W541. Most of that is the erase of blocks of 64 KiB, about a
+ * second each on the W541: three of the store, here, and three of the staging
+ * area by the payload before it; the commit block is erased once, at the end,
+ * as the beginning finds it erased. A swap split over several SMIs would leave a half-written store
  * between them that an S3 resume (the latch of the option lock is taken from
  * the flash again) or another write would meet.
  */
@@ -173,6 +176,23 @@ static bool erase_commit(const struct region_device *rdev, const struct smmstore
 }
 
 #if ENV_SMM
+/*
+ * The commit block erased for a record, read through @ro: a reclaim that ended
+ * erased it already, and an erase of a block is the slow part of a reclaim (one
+ * of 64 KiB takes about a second on the flash of a ThinkPad W541).
+ */
+static bool erase_commit_for_record(const struct region_device *ro, const struct region_device *rw,
+				    const struct smmstore_reclaim_layout *l)
+{
+	for (size_t done = 0; done < SMM_BLOCK_SIZE; done += sizeof(chunk)) {
+		if (rdev_readat(ro, chunk, l->commit + done, sizeof(chunk)) != sizeof(chunk))
+			return erase_commit(rw, l);
+		if (!all_erased(chunk, sizeof(chunk)))
+			return erase_commit(rw, l);
+	}
+	return true;
+}
+
 /* The coreboot options GUID: any GUID gives the walk of the whole store */
 static const EFI_GUID walk_guid = {
 	0xceae4c1d, 0x335b, 0x4685, { 0xa4, 0xa0, 0xfc, 0x4a, 0x94, 0xee, 0xa0, 0x85 } };
@@ -421,7 +441,7 @@ uint32_t smmstore_reclaim(const struct smmstore_params_reclaim *params)
 	record.store_end = l.store_end;
 	record.staging = l.staging;
 	if (!hash_area(&ro, l.staging, l.store_end, record.digest) ||
-	    !erase_commit(&rw, &l) ||
+	    !erase_commit_for_record(&ro, &rw, &l) ||
 	    rdev_writeat(&rw, &record, l.commit, sizeof(record)) != sizeof(record)) {
 		note(&ro, &rw, &l, NOTE_RECORD, 0);
 		return SMMSTORE_RET_FAILURE;
